  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Environmental Degradation and Displacement: Leveraging Citizen-Centred Data to Improve Humanitarian Programming</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">According to the Internal Displacement Monitoring Centre (IDMC), 2021 saw 38 million new displacements, of which 23.7 million &#x2013; almost two thirds &#x2013; were due to natural hazards. These phenomena need to be addressed through a collective approach to prevent adverse effects on populations. In response, IOM and JIPS are jointly leading a Collective Intelligence (CI) initiative targeting DG ECHO&apos;s stated priorities for HIP under (1) disaster displacement and (2) anticipatory action for displacement events related to environment and climate related disasters in the East and Horn of Africa (EHA). This knowledge is being used to feed early warning systems already in place for preventive action. CI is an innovative process by which primary data is gathered from crowds and aggregated through the combination of different analytical approaches to reach conclusions beyond those that could be reached by a single expert. CI has the potential to improve data accuracy and representativeness through greater inclusion and enhanced local and community participation. As a result, this action is contributing to greater global capacity across the humanitarian sector, global governance models, and national authorities for prevention and response planning to disaster displacement.</narrative>
  </description>
